# Staged measured-position pick and place (ROS 2 Humble)

`pick_place_stepwise.py` uses the same `config/task.yaml` and helpers as
`pick_place.py`. Install both scripts alongside one another. Camera detection
and camera calibration are not used to choose pick/place coordinates.

The six prompts are:

1. Open an empty hand, approach above the brick, move to its grasp position.
2. Grasp.
3. Lift by the configured clearance.
4. Move above the destination, then lower to the placement position.
5. Open/release.
6. Retreat above the placed brick, return to the starting arm joint pose.

**Home means the measured starting arm joint pose**, recorded after settling;
it is not a hard-coded Franka ready pose. Start from a known clear pose with an
empty hand. Gripper homing must already have been completed.

Each stage is planned before the prompt. Review the trajectories in RViz.
Enter accepts the stage and, only with `--execute`, runs those exact plans.
For stages containing multiple moves, Enter authorizes all moves described in
that stage. The arm paths are pose-goal plans, not guaranteed straight lines;
inspect the full swept paths. A stage with a planning failure never executes.
One Enter cannot authorize the next stage: pending terminal keys are discarded
before each prompt. Piped input is refused.

The grasp target is the block centre at `fr3_hand_tcp`, fixed tool-down
orientation, with the block aligned to base X/Y and jaw closing along base Y.
Verify actual finger depth and table clearance. Use measured geometry and jaw
settings, not guessed values. The script accepts at most 0.05 velocity and
acceleration scaling; smaller values do not guarantee safe hardware operation.

Hardware mode requires a measured camera/bracket collision object already
attached to `fr3_hand_tcp`, `fr3_hand`, or `fr3_link8` in MoveIt's shared scene.
Its default ID is `handeye_camera` (the hand-eye script's scene command creates
that object when its collision measurements are filled in). An existing object
can be selected using `--camera-object-id ID`. The script adds the configured
table/block to planning requests and preserves other scene obstacles. Represent
all other fixtures as well. It does not automatically publish camera TF.

From `~/Documents/Hackathon/hackathon_ros2_ws`, source ROS Humble and the overlay.
Run with your normal MoveIt stack active and no other competing arm controller.

```bash
# Plan only; Enter advances the virtual sequence without physical execution.
ros2 run fr3_pick_place pick_place_stepwise.py \
  --config src/fr3_pick_place/config/task.yaml

# Hardware: Enter executes each stage. Use only after reviewing the setup.
ros2 run fr3_pick_place pick_place_stepwise.py \
  --config src/fr3_pick_place/config/task.yaml --execute
```

Plan-only mode can use fake hardware. Fake gripper publishing is state-only,
so physical execution mode requires the real gripper action servers.

At prompts, `q` exits. On failure, q, EOF, or Ctrl+C there is no automatic
release or return home: an object could be suspended. Inspect hardware before
retrying. Cancellation during actions uses the original task's best-effort
action cancellation; keep the hardware stop accessible. Each trajectory start
and endpoint is checked against fresh measured joints. Changes while waiting
at a prompt cause an abort. These checks do not replace supervision or account
for obstacles that are absent from the scene or move after planning.

Configuration and camera geometry must be complete before hardware use. There
is no automatic retry/resume after an aborted sequence.
